#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
# netwatch-helper — which program is using the network, measured from packets.
#
# Runs as root. The Netwatch window starts it through pkexec; `sudo netwatch-top`
# runs it in a terminal. It never writes a file and never starts another
# program: it reads /proc and /sys, keeps one TPACKET_V2 ring per network
# interface that holds only the first ~440 bytes of every packet (the headers;
# payload is never copied out of the kernel), and prints one report per
# interval on stdout.
#
# How a packet turns into "Google Chrome  ↓ 1.2 MB/s":
#   packet headers → (protocol, local ip:port, remote ip:port)
#   → socket inode    /proc/net/{tcp,tcp6,udp,udp6}
#   → process         the socket:[inode] links under /proc/<pid>/fd
#   → application     the process's systemd unit, e.g. app-gnome-google\x2dchrome-4242.scope
#
# --json prints one JSON object per line (what the window reads); --text
# redraws a table, and is the default when stdout is a terminal.

